{{#if sandboxing}}
## Bash Sandbox

`bash` commands run in an OS sandbox.

- Reads may access the filesystem, including Git metadata.
{{#if is_linux}}
- `/tmp` is writable but cleared between calls.
{{else}}
{{#if is_windows}}
- Commands run inside WSL under Bubblewrap; native project paths use `/mnt/<drive>/...`.
- `/tmp` inside WSL is writable but cleared between calls.
{{else}}
- The per-thread `$TMPDIR`, `$TMP`, and `$TEMP` are writable and persist across calls.
{{/if}}
{{/if}}
{{#each worktrees}}
- Project root `{{abs_path}}` is writable; protected Git metadata is not.
{{else}}
- No project directory is currently writable.
{{/each}}
- Outbound network access is blocked by default.

Request only the narrow access a command needs:

{{#if is_windows}}
- `allow_all_hosts: true` permits outbound network access. Host-specific grants are unavailable on Windows.
{{else}}
- `allow_hosts: ["host.example"]` permits HTTP/HTTPS to named hosts.
- `allow_all_hosts: true` permits unrestricted outbound network access.
{{/if}}
- `fs_write_paths: ["/path"]` permits writes to existing paths and their subtrees.
- `allow_fs_write_all: true` permits filesystem writes except protected Git metadata.
- `unsandboxed: true` removes the sandbox. Use it only when narrower grants cannot work, including required Git metadata writes.

Prefer `git --no-optional-locks status` for read-only status. A command that must write `.git`, refs, the index, hooks, or worktree metadata needs `unsandboxed: true` with a reason.
{{/if}}

